  "comparison": "At 256x256 RGB (r256), t4-nash-v1 (99.35 ms/step) is 1.56x faster than empf-v8 (155.41 ms/step), 1.31x faster than rgdt-v2 (129.81 ms/step), and 5.93x faster / 2.48x lower-VRAM than rhbi-v12 (588.75 ms/step, 3750.6 MiB); on wide w128 CNNs, 0-SVD scaling (42.18 ms/step) is 1.50x faster than empf-v8 (63.18 ms/step) and 4.22x faster than rhbi-v12 (177.94 ms/step).",
  "conclusion": "Unchanged global SVD-free T4Nash (t4-nash-v1, 0 SVD/eigh/pinv/Newton-Schulz calls, BF16 channels-last) passes all 6/6 dcgan_performance tasks on an uncontended NVIDIA RTX A6000 with finite_update_fraction=1.0: r32=25.99 ms/step (114.6 MiB), r64=32.13 ms/step (303.7 MiB), b64=35.55 ms/step (1789.1 img/s, 396.2 MiB), w128=42.18 ms/step (1029.7 MiB), r128=44.26 ms/step (675.0 MiB), and r256=99.35 ms/step (160.9 img/s, 1512.5 MiB), breaking the 100 ms/step barrier at 256x256.",
